raw_response:
Looking at the error, the `CollatingIterator` requires a comparator to be set. When `null` is passed as the comparator, the `CollatingIterator` doesn't have natural ordering set up automatically.

The fix is to use `ComparatorUtils.naturalComparator()` (or similar) when the comparator is null. Looking at the pattern, when `comparator` is null, we should pass a natural comparator instead.

The fix for all three functions is to replace `null` comparator with `ComparatorUtils.naturalComparator()` when the comparator is null.

```json
{
  "fixed_functions": {
    "1": "public static <E> Iterator<E> collatedIterator(final Comparator<? super E> comparator,
                                                   final Iterator<? extends E> iterator1,
                                                   final Iterator<? extends E> iterator2) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        final Comparator<? super E> comp = comparator == null ? ComparatorUtils.NATURAL_COMPARATOR : comparator;
        return new CollatingIterator<E>(comp, iterator1, iterator2);
    }",
    "2": "public static <E> Iterator<E> collatedIterator(final Comparator<? super E> comparator,
                                                   final Iterator<? extends E>... iterators) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        final Comparator<? super E> comp = comparator == null ? ComparatorUtils.NATURAL_COMPARATOR : comparator;
        return new CollatingIterator<E>(comp, iterators);
    }",
    "3": "public static <E> Iterator<E> collatedIterator(final Comparator<? super E> comparator,
                                                   final Collection<Iterator<? extends E>> iterators) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        final Comparator<? super E> comp = comparator == null ? ComparatorUtils.NATURAL_COMPARATOR : comparator;
        return new CollatingIterator<E>(comp, iterators);
    }"
  }
}
