Looking at the bug, the issue is that `-0.0` is being printed as `0` instead of `-0.0`.

The condition `(long) x == x` checks if `x` is a whole number. For `-0.0`, `(long) -0.0` equals `0L`, and `0L == -0.0` is `true` in Java (since `-0.0 == 0.0` in floating point). So `-0.0` takes the integer path and gets printed as `0`.

The fix needs to handle the special case of `-0.0` (negative zero). We can detect negative zero by checking `x == 0.0 && (1/x) == Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY`, or more simply by checking `x != 0` before the cast comparison, or by using `Double.doubleToRawLongBits` to distinguish `-0.0` from `0.0`.

The simplest fix is to add `x != 0` to the condition (since `-0.0 == 0.0` in Java, but we want to treat `-0.0` as not an integer for printing purposes), or check that `1/x != Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY`.

The cleanest approach: change the condition to also exclude negative zero by checking `1/x != Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY` when `x == 0`, or more simply check `Double.doubleToRawLongBits(x) != Double.doubleToRawLongBits(-0.0)`.

