raw_response:
Looking at the bug, I need to understand what happens when the type id comes before the POJO in the JSON (the "fail case").

**Analysis:**

The bug is in this section of the code:
```java
if (creatorProp != null) {
    // first: let's check to see if this might be part of value with external type id:
    if (ext.handlePropertyValue(p, ctxt, propName, buffer)) { /* bug is here */
        ;
    } else {
        // Last creator property to set?
        if (buffer.assignParameter(...)) { ... }
    }
    continue;
}
```

When `creatorProp != null`, the code calls `ext.handlePropertyValue(p, ctxt, propName, buffer)` passing `buffer` as the last argument. According to the comment just above this call:
```
// 11-Sep-2015, tatu: Important; do NOT pass buffer as last arg, but null,
//   since it is not the bean
```

The comment says to pass `null`, not `buffer`! But the code passes `buffer` instead.

When `buffer` is passed, `ExternalTypeHandler.handlePropertyValue` treats it as a bean and tries to call `deserializeAndSet` on a `CreatorProperty`, which doesn't support that operation (no fallback setter/field), causing the `IllegalStateException`.

The fix is to pass `null` instead of `buffer` as the last argument, matching what the comment says should happen, and then handle the creator property in the `else` branch as intended.

